# Pocket Assistant sdkconfig overlay — Seeed XIAO ESP32-S3.
#
# Layered LAST, after config/sdkconfig.defaults, boards/xiao_s3.defaults,
# config/sdkconfig.ota.defaults and boards/<board>.ota.defaults, so the values
# here win.  It applies to the main application only; the recovery updater is a
# separate project and takes its configuration from updater/boards/xiao_s3.defaults.
#
# Deployments describe policy, boards describe hardware.  This file exists
# because ONE piece of this deployment's policy has to be expressed in
# sdkconfig rather than in features.h.
#
# boards/xiao_s3.defaults sets CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=n, which was correct for the
# standalone XIAO image it was written for (an on-device-LLM build that
# overflowed its factory partition by 0x3ff0 with Bluedroid linked in).  That
# is a per-image trade, not a property of the silicon — the S3 has a BLE radio.
#
# This is load-bearing, and it fails SILENTLY if removed.  The derived rules in
# System_BuildConfig.h and the mirrored downgrade in
# components/hardwareone/CMakeLists.txt both force ENABLE_BLUETOOTH to 0 when
# the active sdkconfig has no Bluedroid, and ENABLE_G2_GLASSES / ENABLE_R1_HEALTH
# follow it down.  The result would be a green build of a "Pocket Assistant"
# with no glasses, no ring and no BLE — which is the entire product.
#
# The other three keys are already y in config/sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3;
# they are restated so this overlay is a complete, readable statement of the
# BT contract instead of a single line that only makes sense next to another file.
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_CONTROLLER_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLUEDROID_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLE_42_FEATURES_SUPPORTED=y
